course: politics
lesson: 38
title: Protests, petitions and young activists
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: There are many peaceful ways to ask for change: a protest, where people gather in public with signs, songs and speeches; a petition, a list of names asking for the same thing; and a polite letter to your MP.
explain: Picture the playground: if lots of kids sign a paper asking for a new bench and take it to the head, she is more likely to listen than if one kid moans alone; that is how a petition works, only bigger.
explain: Young people have started big things: Greta Thunberg's school strike for climate, Malala Yousafzai speaking up for girls' education, students organising the March for Our Lives; many admire them and many disagree with what they ask for.
explain: Across the road there may be a counter-protest with the opposite view, and both sides are allowed to protest peacefully; neither is "the baddies" just for disagreeing.
explain: Smashing windows or hurting people is against the law even when the cause is good, and children go to marches only with a trusted adult and a safety plan.
fact: A protest is a public gathering to show you want something changed or stopped; peaceful protest is allowed by law in countries like the UK, USA, Canada, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland, with police rules on where and when.
fact: A petition is a written request signed by many people, and on some governments' petition websites a big petition can get a reply or a debate in parliament.
fact: An MP represents everyone in their area, including children who cannot vote yet, and writing politely is a real way to be heard.
fact: As widely reported, Greta Thunberg began a school strike for climate in 2018 at 15, and millions of young people later joined climate strikes.
fact: As widely reported, Malala Yousafzai spoke up for girls' education in Pakistan, was attacked by the Taliban in 2012, survived, and won the Nobel Peace Prize at 17.
fact: As widely reported, after a school shooting in Parkland, Florida in 2018, students organised the March for Our Lives to ask for stricter gun laws, and other Americans disagreed with what they asked for.
fact: Some say young activists are told what to say by adults; others say young people think for themselves; both can be partly true.
fact: Children at a march should stay with a trusted adult, agree a meeting point, keep a phone charged and leave if it gets too crowded.
fact: In big protests most people may be peaceful while a few break things, and damage and violence are against the law even when the cause is good.
fact: A counter-protest is a protest by people who disagree with another protest, and police often keep the two groups apart.
fact: On climate, some say action must be fast because waiting makes it worse; others say changes that come too fast can cost jobs and make fuel too expensive.
fact: Words in this lesson: "slay" means do something brilliantly; "back in my day" is how older people start a story about when they were young; "yinz" in Pittsburgh means you all; "craic" in Ireland means fun or news, and "eejit" means fool.
exercise: Noah sees a crowd marching with signs. Dad says it's not a fight. What is it? | A protest | A street riot | A football match | A secret ballot
exercise: In countries like the UK, USA and Australia, is peaceful protest allowed? | Yes, by law, though police can set rules on where and when | No, all protests are against the law, and anyone who joins one is arrested | Only if the government agrees with the protest | Only on weekends and only for adults
exercise: Chloe collects 300 signatures asking for a crossing outside school. What has she made? | A petition | A referendum | A bribe for votes | A counter-protest
exercise: What can a big petition on a government petition website lead to? | A reply or even a debate in parliament | An automatic new law the next day | A fine for everyone who signed | Nothing, petitions are never read
exercise: Ruby, who is too young to vote, writes to her MP. Does the MP represent her? | Yes, an MP represents everyone in the area, including children | No, MPs only represent the people who voted for them at the last election | No, MPs only represent adults | No, MPs only read letters from businesses
exercise: How did Greta Thunberg start her climate campaign, as widely reported? | By skipping school on Fridays to sit outside parliament with a sign | By becoming prime minister of Sweden | By starting a TV channel about the weather that showed every storm in the world as it happened | By writing a law about cars
exercise: What did Malala Yousafzai speak up for? | Girls going to school | Building more motorways | Lower prices for sweets | Longer school holidays
exercise: What did students ask for at the March for Our Lives? | Stricter gun laws | Longer lunch breaks | Free pizza in schools | More homework every day
exercise: A radio host says young activists are "just being used by grown-ups". What is the fairest view? | Young people learn from adults and also form their own views | Children never have their own opinions until they are old enough to vote at eighteen | Adults never influence children at all | Only adults are allowed to have views
exercise: What safety plan did Oisin and his mum make for the march? | Stay together, a meeting point, phone charged, leave if too crowded | Go alone and meet at home afterwards | Stand at the very front of the crowd, where the police can see them and keep them safe | Leave phones at home to keep them safe
exercise: Some people at a protest smash windows while others tell them to stop. What is true? | Damage is against the law even when the cause is good | Smashing windows is fine as long as the cause is a good one | Everyone at the protest broke windows, even the ones shouting | Nobody at the protest was peaceful, so the whole march is to blame
exercise: What is a counter-protest? | A protest by people who disagree with another protest | A protest held at a shop counter | A protest that counts the marchers | A protest by police officers
exercise: Kai asks if the "Save our jobs" group across the road are the baddies. What is the best answer? | No, they disagree and are also allowed to protest peacefully | Yes, anyone who disagrees is a baddie | Yes, only the bigger group is allowed to protest, so the smaller one across the road is breaking the law | No, because they are not really protesting
exercise: What do the two sides of the climate argument say? | Act fast because waiting makes it worse, or go slower to protect jobs and prices | Everyone agrees on exactly what to do and only argues about which day to start doing it | Nobody thinks climate matters at all | Both sides want to stop all cars tomorrow
exercise: Why do police often keep a protest and a counter-protest apart? | So arguments do not turn into fights | To stop either side from being seen on TV | To count which side is bigger and louder | Because protests are against the law there
test: "Back in my day we just wrote to our MP, now kids think a TikTok's protest" — Ruby wrote a polite letter and got a reply. What does that show? | Writing to your MP is still a real way to be heard | MPs only answer TikToks now | Letters to MPs are against the rules unless a grown-up signs them and posts them for you | Only grandparents can write to MPs
test: "Greta just slays, she's always right" vs "she should've stayed in school". What is a fair summary? | Many admire her and many disagree with her | Everyone agrees with everything she says | Nobody has ever disagreed with her | She was never really a school student
test: In Pittsburgh: "Yinz wanna sign? We need a crossing outside the school." What is being asked? | To add your name to a petition | To vote in a referendum | To join a counter-protest | To give money to a candidate
test: What would happen if Oisin got split from his cousin at a big march? | They would go to the meeting point they agreed | He would just stay wherever he was | He would follow the loudest group | He would go to the front of the crowd
test: The news calls a protest "mostly peaceful with some violence". A boy says, "So every single person there was violent." What is better? | Most were peaceful while a few broke things | Everyone there broke windows | Nobody there broke anything | The news made the whole protest up
test: In Dublin: "Great craic at the march, but sure the other lot across the road are eejits." What is the fairer view? | The other group disagrees and is also allowed to protest peacefully | Anyone across the road must be wrong, because the bigger march is always the one that is right | Counter-protests are always against the law | Only one side may protest at a time
test: A boy says, "Malala won a Nobel Prize for winning a football match." What is the right version? | She won the Nobel Peace Prize for speaking up for girls' education | She won it for swimming the fastest | She never won any prize | She won it for inventing a phone that lets girls in faraway villages call their schools
test: In a cartoon, the town mice line up with signs asking the mayor for a cheese shop, and nobody breaks anything. What is that like? | A peaceful protest | A riot | A bribe | A deepfake
test: "they're just kids, adults tell 'em what to say, end of" — what is a fairer view? | Young people learn from adults and also think for themselves | Children never think for themselves, so everything a young activist says was written by a grown-up | Adults never influence anyone | Kids are banned from having views
test: The march was started after a school shooting in Florida. Did all Americans agree with what it asked for? | No, other Americans disagreed with stricter gun laws | Yes, every American agreed, and the gun laws were changed the very next morning | Nobody in America heard about it | The march asked for more guns
test: "petitions are pointless, it's just names" — what is the best answer? | Many names can show leaders that lots of people care | Petitions automatically become laws | Petitions are only for grown-ups | Names on paper are against the law
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a petition? | A written request subscribed by many persons seeking the same outcome | A ballot in which the whole nation answers a single question put to it by the government of the day | A gathering in public of persons opposed to a measure | A gift conferred in exchange for a vote
exam: Identify the person by whom a child too young to vote is represented in a national parliament. | The member of parliament for the area in which the child resides | No person, until the child attains voting age | The head teacher of the child's school | The member of parliament chosen by the child's parents only, at the election preceding the child's birth
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning damage to property during a protest. | It is unlawful irrespective of the merit of the cause advanced | It is lawful where the cause advanced is meritorious | It is lawful where the majority of protesters are peaceful and the damage is modest | It is lawful where it is reported in the news
exam: Whereas a counter-protest is held opposite a march, determine the statement most accurately characterising the two assemblies. | Each may protest peacefully, and each believes it protects something of importance | Only the larger assembly is entitled to protest | The counter-protest is unlawful by its very nature | The original march is unlawful by reason of the counter-protest, which takes precedence over it in law
